#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230063} :activity/id #uuid "a0d14e2e-e747-5040-8791-ce5508f25e94" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048194 :topic/id #uuid "8738db80-c159-5ab6-867d-b5328c8d4b41" :topic/math-academy-id 6718} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230063 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230233 :tutorial/content "In this topic, we will use Laplace transforms to handle **time-delayed forcing**, where the forcing function turns on at some time $t=a>0$.\n\nWe represent this “turning on” using the **unit step function** $u_a(t)=u(t-a)$.\n\nTo see what this looks like, consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' - 4y = u_2(t)\\cdot e^{t-2}, \\qquad y(0)=0.\n$$\n\nHere, the forcing is $0$ for $t<2$ and becomes $e^{t-2}$ for $t\\ge 2$.\n\nTo find the Laplace transform of the forcing term, we note that\n\n$$\nu_2(t)\\cdot e^{t-2}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=2$ and $f(t)=e^{t-2}.$\n\nNow, recall that the **second shifting theorem** (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\} = e^{-as} \\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-2)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}\\} & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L\\{{e}^{(t+2)-2}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L\\{{e}^{t}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter this step, we proceed as usual: we take the Laplace transform of the entire differential equation, solve for $Y(s)=\\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$, and then (when needed) take $\\mathcal L^{-1}$ to recover $y(t)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "a57da068-df6c-54ea-98c8-714805c81391" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14744 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "33e4bd2f-e595-58ed-9a93-727ced833dc4" :step/math-academy-id 600519 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230064}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230064 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230231 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' - 4y = u_2(t)\\cdot e^{t-2}, \\qquad y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $u_2(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\nFirst, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the linearity properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}-4y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}-4L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $\\mathcal L\\{y'\\},$ we use the derivative formula and the initial condition $y(0)=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{y'\\} = sY(s) - y(0) = sY(s).\n$$\n\nTo compute the right-hand side, we note that\n\n$$\nu_2(t)\\cdot e^{t-2}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=2$ and $f(t)=e^{t-2}.$\n\nNow, recall that the **second shifting theorem** (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}=e^{-as}\\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-2)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}\\} & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L\\{{e}^{(t+2)-2}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L\\{{e}^{t}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation and solve for $Y(s){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sY(s)-4Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s-1} \\\\ (s-4)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s-1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{(s-1)(s-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s)$ will give us the solution to our initial value problem." :tutorial/id #uuid "3066f75f-f843-5283-a3be-923fa13ce2dd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14715 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "c79dc0af-fb98-5c4a-95f9-e6bf263d96dc" :step/math-academy-id 600520 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230065}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230065 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229171 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228946 :question/id #uuid "89706c7f-b9a4-5d5d-84e8-2c6df19db4ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-21860"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed252518-aeda-53dc-a900-ce6981c78c16" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049492}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565481}] :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of First-Order Linear ODEs With Time-Delayed (Discontinuous) Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "e11300e8-c33d-57ec-95a3-cbca3b7d9c73" :step/math-academy-id 600521 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230067}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230066 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229172 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228947 :question/id #uuid "07f59403-66ac-531c-81d3-e286231595ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-21861"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f316163e-7e1e-5c7f-a75d-9c917dd8bcda" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050768}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565466}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order Initial Value Problem Using a Laplace Transform"} :step/id #uuid "f45c78a8-04bf-5a0d-9b2a-da4fc8eb54e6" :step/math-academy-id 600525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230067 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230234 :tutorial/content "When working with time-delayed forcing, a delay often appears in the $s$-domain as a factor of $e^{-as}$.\n\nSuppose $F(s)=\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}.$ Then the second shifting theorem can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\} = e^{-as}\\,F(s), \\qquad a>0.\n$$\n\nEquivalently, we can read this rule *backwards* as an inverse Laplace transform statement:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L^{-1}\\{e^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a), \\qquad a>0.\n$$\n\nSo, if $Y(s)$ contains a term like $e^{-as}F(s)$, we can handle it using the following steps.\n\n- **Step 1:** Compute $f(t)=\\mathcal L^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$.\n\n- **Step 2:** Shift the function by replacing $t$ with $(t-a)$.\n\n- **Step 3:** Multiply by the unit step function $u(t-a)$.\n\nWe consider the concrete example below." :tutorial/id #uuid "f9ee51be-d7c5-57bd-9302-41618fa57ab0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14745 :tutorial/title "Taking the Inverse Transform and Applying the Second Shifting Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "03a9dbab-bfb2-5a17-8b2a-da80a0acc614" :step/math-academy-id 600529 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230100}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230100 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230240 :tutorial/content "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' + 3y = u_\\pi(t)\\cdot \\cos(t-\\pi)\\,, \\qquad y(0)=2,\n$$\n\nwhere $u_\\pi(t)=u(t-\\pi)$ is the unit step function.\n\nAssume we are given that the Laplace transform $Y(s) = \\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s) = \\dfrac{2}{s+3} + \\dfrac{s\\,e^{-\\pi s}}{(s^2+1)(s+3)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t) = 2e^{-3t} + \\dfrac{1}{10} u(t-\\pi)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nOur goal is to find the function $g(t).$ To do this, we'll need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the second term in $Y(s).$ We can use the following standard results:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\sin\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}, \\qquad \n\\mathcal L\\{\\cos\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}, \\qquad\ns > 0.\n$$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential $e^{-\\pi s}$ and identify its rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{s}{(s^2+1)(s+3)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll find the inverse transform in three steps.\n\n**Step 1. Partial fraction decomposition**\n\nTo handle the rational function $F(s),$ we use *partial fraction decomposition*. Since the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+3)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $(s^2+1),$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{s}{(s^2+1)(s+3)} = \\dfrac{A}{s+3} + \\dfrac{Bs+C}{s^2+1}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns = A(s^2+1) + (Bs+C)(s+3).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A, B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n-3 = A\\bigl((-3)^2+1\\bigr) + 0 \\implies 10A = -3 \\implies A = -\\dfrac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of $s^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n0 = A + B \\implies B = -A = \\dfrac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n1 = 3B + C \\implies 1 = 3\\!\\left(\\dfrac{3}{10}\\right) + C \\implies C = \\dfrac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)= -\\dfrac{3}{10}\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{s+3} + \\dfrac{3}{10}\\cdot\\dfrac{s}{s^2+1} + \\dfrac{1}{10}\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{s^2+1}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2. Finding the inverse of the base function**\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t) = \\mathcal L^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{-\\frac{3}{10}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+3}+\\frac{3}{10}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}+\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}\\} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{10}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(-3{e}^{-3t}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3. Applying the time-shifting property**\n\nFinally, we use the *second shifting theorem* to handle the $e^{-\\pi s}$ factor. The theorem states:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L^{-1} \\{ e^{-as}F(s) \\} = u(t-a) \\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\pi.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-\\pi):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-\\pi )f(t-\\pi ) & =u(t-\\pi )\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[-3{e}^{-3(t-\\pi )}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\pi )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}u(t-\\pi )\\cdot [-3{e}^{-3(t-\\pi )}+3(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identities $\\cos(t-\\pi)=-\\cos t$ and $\\sin(t-\\pi)=-\\sin t,$ we simplify:\n\n$$\nu(t-\\pi)f(t-\\pi) = \\dfrac{1}{10}u(t-\\pi)\\cdot \\left(-3e^{-3(t-\\pi)} - 3\\cos t - \\sin t\\right).\n$$\n\n**Conclusion: Identifying g(t)**\n\nThis result is the inverse transform of the second term in $Y(s).$ The problem statement gives the complete solution in the form\n\n$$\ny(t) = 2e^{-3t} + \\dfrac{1}{10} u(t-\\pi)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nThe first term, $2e^{-3t},$ is the inverse transform of $\\dfrac{2}{s+3}.$ By comparing our calculated term with the form above, we can identify **$g(t)$**:\n\n$$\ng(t)= -3e^{-3(t-\\pi)} - 3\\cos t - \\sin t.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ea2c953f-32af-54a8-abd8-8f2f9a59c107" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14882 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "c822adae-6fde-5b63-ae68-0fa00820fcc7" :step/math-academy-id 607925 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230066}}] :activity/title "Solving First-Order ODEs With Time-Delayed Forcing Using Laplace Transforms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186229269}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565524} :activity/id #uuid "a214e70e-a89f-5501-afbb-0c4c47f76175" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048194 :topic/id #uuid "8738db80-c159-5ab6-867d-b5328c8d4b41" :topic/math-academy-id 6718} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565488 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565481 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6d68d2a-0474-5f05-8194-03e94a84b4a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5{e}^{-3s}}{(s-2)(s+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565238}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565238} :answer-field/id #uuid "e48a5a69-87f8-5b78-872f-f1525fb7b704" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f427f52d-d8d0-506f-a966-8597af9a76fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-342538" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=5{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{2t-6}\\,,\\,y(0)=5,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{3}(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function.\n\n$Y(s)=\\frac{5}{s+2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>2$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}+2y\\} & =L\\{5{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{2t-6}\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+2L\\{y\\} & =L\\{5{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{2t-6}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=5:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)-5\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n5{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{2t-6}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=3$ and $f(t)=5{e}^{2t-6}.$\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{5u(t-3)\\cdot {e}^{2t-6}\\} & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot L\\{5{e}^{2(t+3)-6}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot L\\{5{e}^{2t}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{5}{s-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{e}^{-3s}}{s-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+2L\\{y\\} & =L\\{5{u}_{3}(t){e}^{2t-6}\\} \\\\ (sY(s)-5)+2Y(s) & =\\frac{5{e}^{-3s}}{s-2} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s)-5 & =\\frac{5{e}^{-3s}}{s-2} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s) & =5+\\frac{5{e}^{-3s}}{s-2} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{5}{s+2}+\\frac{5{e}^{-3s}}{(s-2)(s+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0dcdb20b-ebe0-5c21-b7b8-023e7ee38c1e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565510}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565499 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eb0f356-7ea1-5211-98fe-5b84dd6ba27d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}(s+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315294} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba17ec9-0c67-56cc-950e-450d8cd0068d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12e^{-2s}}{s^4(s+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565126}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565126} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd5c0207-5c3d-553c-af70-1c15c3661a5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "97923b3c-8f90-512a-b7ec-bb8a53b3d22c" :question/math-academy-id "q-342532" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\n$Y(s)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}+4y\\} & =L\\{2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+4L\\{y\\} & =L\\{2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=2$ and $f(t)=2(t-2)^{3}.$\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{2u(t-2)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}\\} & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L\\{2((t+2)-2)^{3}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L\\{2{t}^{3}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{3!}{{s}^{3+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+4L\\{y\\} & =L\\{2{u}_{2}(t)(t-2)^{3}\\} \\\\ sY(s)+4Y(s) & =\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}(s+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3333a917-cfff-58f6-aefa-a4cb333c9354" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565488}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565510 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565466 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "072c003f-940f-5827-9c69-c6534a1061e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564952} {:answer/id #uuid "25a59e37-3156-5f96-a6d3-a085278a86ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564997} {:answer/id #uuid "638d7ca2-b9ee-56e3-ad94-7e22ab34a1e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565092} {:answer/id #uuid "ac923911-04d7-59bb-9992-7a8446c5e934" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565194} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a09306-4232-5e19-a749-d592df6f358b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565236}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565092} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d53ce85-93a3-5040-89c6-3357d95d5db8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d1348d32-c72c-5639-a597-5810a3541a7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-342800" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+2y={u}_{\\pi /3}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(3t-\\pi )\\,,\\,y(0)=-1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /3}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=-\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{s\\,{e}^{-\\pi s/3}}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+2)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=-{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{13}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$ \n\nHint: You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-\\pi s/3}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+2)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+2)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+9),$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+2)}=\\frac{A}{s+2}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+9}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns=A({s}^{2}+9)+(Bs+C)(s+2).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n-2=A((-2)^{2}+9)+0\\,⟹\\,13A=-2\\,⟹\\,A=-\\frac{2}{13}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,B=-A=\\frac{2}{13}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n\n$$\n0=9A+2C\\,⟹\\,0=9\\,(-\\frac{2}{13})+2C\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{9}{13}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{13}(-\\frac{2}{s+2}+\\frac{2s+9}{{s}^{2}+9})=\\frac{1}{13}(-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+2}+2\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}+3\\cdot \\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{13}(-2\\cdot {L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s+2}\\}+2\\cdot {L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}\\}+3\\cdot {L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}\\}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(-2{e}^{-2t}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-\\pi s/3}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})f\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}[-2{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}))+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}))].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identities $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(t-\\pi /3))=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-\\pi /3))=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),$ we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}(-2{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{13}\\,u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})(2{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=-{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{13}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=2{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "68e12a72-bb50-5a7a-a965-62ff8a107f26" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565513}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565513 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c9f894b-0695-5fb5-a2fc-03e32b1ccede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565009} {:answer/id #uuid "61321b40-2ea0-5afa-9cb1-4cc94c8b9dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565088} {:answer/id #uuid "9c669607-1843-5584-8f41-4389bb11e66b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565176} {:answer/id #uuid "b380e63b-0a3b-5d84-8474-0490488b54a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565206} {:answer/id #uuid "eec60117-16db-52c1-b4e7-87394fffcc2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565298}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565088} :answer-field/id #uuid "2235b94a-23b2-515a-9cb2-13dc47980c69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9876167b-d3d3-5f4c-9305-b455d21b66dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-342816" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-3y={u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(4t-\\pi )\\,,\\,y(0)=1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /4}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{1}{s-3}+\\frac{s\\,{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{({s}^{2}+16)(s-3)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{3t}+\\frac{1}{25}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$ \n\nHint: You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the initial value problem, we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s).$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first ignore the exponential term ${e}^{-\\pi s/4}$ (which corresponds to a time shift) and focus on the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+16)(s-3)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s-3)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+16),$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+16)(s-3)}=\\frac{A}{s-3}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+16}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns=A({s}^{2}+16)+(Bs+C)(s-3).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n3=A({3}^{2}+16)+0\\,⟹\\,25A=3\\,⟹\\,A=\\frac{3}{25}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,B=-A=-\\frac{3}{25}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n\n$$\n0=16A-3C\\,⟹\\,0=16\\,(\\frac{3}{25})-3C\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{16}{25}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{25}(\\frac{3}{s-3}-3\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}+4\\cdot \\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{25}(3\\cdot {L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-3}\\}-3\\cdot {L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}\\}+4\\cdot {L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}\\}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}(3{e}^{3t}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-\\pi s/4}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})f\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot \\frac{1}{25}[3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identities $cos\\,(4\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$ and $sin\\,(4\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t),$ we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot \\frac{1}{25}(3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\,u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})(3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)={e}^{3t}+\\frac{1}{25}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "75507dff-4f4d-5b89-a3f1-7cd41cb6835c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565524 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229740 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e7b9c61-3e75-5f82-92cf-67afbe5b5737" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{-(t-1)}-{e}^{-2(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564962} {:answer/id #uuid "7aa19d89-4802-59c5-aa41-c5e4815bad9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-(t-1)}-{e}^{-(t-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565123} {:answer/id #uuid "acd86ce9-6dce-5fe4-9b28-94ed59469b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-(t-1)}-{e}^{-2(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565195} {:answer/id #uuid "b3718138-b1f7-5833-85d1-e340c7cd8e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565204} {:answer/id #uuid "ea7ae7d0-cc80-5360-b492-20f511b7f34d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{-(t-1)}+{e}^{-2(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565195} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1294a04-a4d7-549c-ab04-a206d685e72c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "81cfd049-da26-58a2-97d8-8fcbe6ff442d" :question/math-academy-id "q-342315" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+y={u}_{1}(t)\\cdot {e}^{-2(t-1)}\\,,\\,y(0)=1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}(t)=u(t-1)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{1}{s+1}+\\frac{{e}^{-s}}{(s+1)(s+2)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{-t}+u(t-1)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{(s+1)(s+2)}\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains distinct linear factors, the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(s+1)(s+2)}=\\frac{A}{s+1}+\\frac{B}{s+2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=A(s+2)+B(s+1)\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Setting $s=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(-1+2)+B(0)\\,⟹\\,A=1.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(-2+1)\\,⟹\\,B=-1.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s+2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s+2}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}-{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=1.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-1)f(t-1) & =u(t-1)[{e}^{-(t-1)}-{e}^{-2(t-1)}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)={e}^{-t}+u(t-1)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{-(t-1)}-{e}^{-2(t-1)}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a64c28fc-1ca6-5640-89a6-d8051d9dde56" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565499}}] :activity/title "Solving First-Order ODEs With Time-Delayed Forcing Using Laplace Transforms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564934}]}
